<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bi Sheng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1051">1051</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bi Sheng was a Chinese artisan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> credited with inventing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/movable-type">movable type</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> using ceramic characters around 1040 CE. His method is recorded in the writings of the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-kuo">Shen Kuo</a>. The invention predated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johannes-gutenberg">Gutenberg</a>'s press by about four centuries and is counted among the major technological achievements of Song <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bi-sheng.jpg" alt="Bi Sheng" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Popolon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beijing.Musee_imprimerie.caracteres_mobiles.Bisheng.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Bi Sheng</b> invented movable type using ceramic characters around 1040, some four centuries before Gutenberg's printing press.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tangut script, attributed to Yeli Renrong and based on Chinese and Khitan, was used from 1036 to translate and print Chinese and Tibetan works, and Western Xia pioneered movable type after <b>Bi Sheng</b> (972–1051): the Auspicious Tantra of All-Reaching Union is the earliest surviving wooden-type book.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…names are a king under whom a thing was made and an artisan whose method a scholar wrote down: Hangul was created in 1443 under Sejong the Great of Joseon, and the movable type that <b>Bi Sheng</b> made about 1040 is recorded in the writings of Shen Kuo.</blockquote><a href="/essays/who-invented-it-first">Who Invented It First?</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1040 the artisan <b>Bi Sheng</b> invented printing with movable clay type, as the scholar Shen Kuo recorded in his Dream Pool Essays.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li></ul></section>
